ON THE PLAN'S STEP 4 CONSISTENCY CHECK

  Step 4 says to compare iesmap_entry_count() against

    snmpwalk -v2c -c public -On <dut> 1.3.6.1.4.1.8691 | wc -l

  Those are not the same quantity. snmpwalk counts OID *instances* - a table
  column over 12 ports is 12 lines - while iesmap_entry_count() counts table
  *entries*, one per column. The two cannot be expected to match and a
  mismatch would prove nothing.

  What Step 2 was actually guarding against is the two sides being compiled
  with different macro sets. That is now structural: both sides compile the
  same MOX_SNMP_INIT_ENTRY sequence from the same file with the same -D set,
  taken from one variable. 1936 is recorded above as the number this build
  produces, so a future change that moves it is visible.

LOOKUP SEMANTICS

  Longest-prefix match. A GET carries a full instance OID (column plus index)
  while an entry names only its column, so an entry matches when its OID is a
  prefix of the query and the longest such entry wins. The test checks that the
  column OID and the same OID with an index appended resolve to one entry.

  Linear scan, as the plan asks. No index, no cache, no hash until something
  measures that 1936 entries are too slow.

NOT DONE

  Nothing on the DUT. This task produces no runtime behaviour change - the
  library is not linked into any executable until Task 4 wraps it for Rust and
  Task 5 uses it. snmpd is byte-identical, so there is nothing to re-measure.
